      "topic_confidence": 0.575,
      "topic_reason": [
        "physics keyword scorer v2"
      ],
      "question_text": "A student investigates how light can be used to measure the speed of a toy train.\n \ndirection of travel\nlaser light\ndouble slit\n1.5  mm\n light sensor\n \n5.0  m\ntoy train\n(not to scale)\n \nLight from a laser is incident on a double slit. The light from the slits is detected by a \nlight sensor attached to the train.\n \nThe graph shows the variation with time of the output voltage from the light sensor as the \ntrain moves parallel to the slits. The output voltage is proportional to the intensity of light \nincident on the sensor.\noutput voltage\n0\n 0 \n25 \n50 \n75 \n100\ntime / ms\n(This question continues on the following page)\n28EP10\n\nM17/4/PHYSI/HP2/ENG/TZ2/XX\n– 11 –\nTurn over \n(Question 4 continued)\n \n(a) \nExplain, with reference to the light passing through the slits, why a series of \nvoltage peaks occurs.\n[3]\n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n(b) \n(i) \nThe slits are separated by 1.5  mm and the laser light has a wavelength \nof 6.3   10–7  m. The slits are 5.0  m from the train track. Calculate the separation \nbetween two adjacent positions of the train when the output voltage is at \na maximum.\n[1]\n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n \n(ii) \nEstimate the speed of the train.\n[2]\n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n(This question continues on the following page)\n28EP11\n\nM17/4/PHYSI/HP2/ENG/TZ2/XX\n– 12 –\n(Question 4 continued)\n \n(c) \nAs the train continues to move, the first diffraction minimum is observed when the \nlight sensor is at a distance of 0.13  m from the centre of the fringe pattern.\n \ndirection of travel\nfirst diffraction minimum\n0.13  m\ncentre of fringe pattern\ntoy train\n(not to scale)\n \n \n(i) \nDetermine the width of one of the slits.\n[2]\n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .