      "question_text": "A cable consisting of many copper wires is used to transfer electrical energy from an \nalternating current (ac) generator to an electrical load. The copper wires are protected by \nan insulator.\ncopper wires\n \ninsulator\n \n(a) \nThe copper wires and insulator are both exposed to an electric field. Discuss, \nwith reference to charge carriers, why there is a significant electric current only in \nthe copper wires.\n[3]\n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n(This question continues on the following page)\n28EP19\n\nM17/4/PHYSI/HP2/ENG/TZ2/XX\n– 20 –\n(Question 6 continued)\n \n(b) \nThe cable consists of 32 copper wires each of length 35  km. Each wire has a \nresistance of 64  Ω. The cable is connected to the ac generator which has an \noutput power of 110  MW when the peak potential difference is 150  kV. The resistivity \nof copper is 1.7   10–8  Ω  m.\noutput power  =  110  MW\n ac generator\n \nelectrical load\n \ncable\n \nreturn path for current\n \n \n(i) \nCalculate the radius of each wire.\n[2]\n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n \n(ii) \nCalculate the peak current in the cable.\n[1]\n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n \n(iii) \nDetermine the power dissipated in the cable per unit length.\n[3]\n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n(This question continues on the following page)\n28EP20\n\nM17/4/PHYSI/HP2/ENG/TZ2/XX\n– 21 –\nTurn over \n(Question 6 continued)\n \n(c) \nTo ensure that the power supply cannot be interrupted, two identical cables are \nconnected in parallel.\n ac generator\n \nelectrical load\n \ncable\n \ncable\n \nreturn path for current\n \n \nCalculate the root mean square (rms) current in each cable.\n[1]\n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n(d) \nThe two cables in part (c) are suspended a constant distance apart. Explain how the \nmagnetic forces acting between the cables vary during the course of one cycle of the \nalternating current (ac).\n[2]\n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n(This question continues on page 23)\n28EP21\n\nM17/4/PHYSI/HP2/ENG/TZ2/XX\n– 22 –\nPlease do not write on this page.\nAnswers written on this page will not \nbe marked.\n28EP22\n\nM17/4/PHYSI/HP2/ENG/TZ2/XX\n– 23 –\nTurn over \n(Question 6 continued from page 21)\n \n(e) \nThe energy output of the ac generator is at a much lower voltage than the 150  kV used \nfor transmission. A step-up transformer is used between the generator and the cables.\n \n \n(i) \nSuggest the advantage of using a step-up transformer in this way.\n[2]\n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n \n(ii) \nThe use of alternating current (ac) in a transformer gives rise to energy losses. \nState how eddy current loss is minimized in the transformer.\n[1]\n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n28EP23\n\nM17/4/PHYSI/HP2/ENG/TZ2/XX\n– 24 –",
      "answer_text": "a \nwhen an electric field is applied to any material «using a cell etc» it \nacts to accelerate any free electrons  \n3 \n \nelectrons are the charge carriers «in copper»  \n \nmetals/copper have many free electrons whereas insulators have \nfew/no free electrons/charge carriers  \n \nb \ni \narea\n8\n3\n6\n2\n1.7 10\n35 10\n \n9.3 10\n m\n64\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n«\n» \n2 \nradius\n6\n9.3 10\n \n0.00172 m\n\n\n\n\n\n«\n»\n\nb \nii \npeak\npeak\npeak\n730\nA\nP\nV\n\n«\n»\n«\n»\nI\n  \n\n1 \n(continued…) \n\n– 18 –\nM17/4/PHYSI/HP2/ENG/TZ2/XX/M \n(Question 6 continued) \nQuestion \nAnswers \nNotes \nTotal \nb \niii \nresistance of cable identified as\n64\n2\n32 \n\n«\n»\n  \n3 \na power\n35000 seen in solution \nplausible answer calculated using \n2\n2I\n «plausible if in\n35000\nrange 10 W m–1 to 150 W m–1 when quoted answers in \n(b)(ii) used» 31 «W m–1»ü \nAllow [3] for a solution where the resistance per unit metre is \ncalculated using resistivity and answer to (a) (resistance per unit \nlength of cable 5.710–5 m ) \nAward [2 max] if 64  used for resistance (answer32). \nV 2\nor VI using 150 kV is incorrect (award\nR\n[0]), however allow this approach if the pd across the cable has \nbeen calculated (pd dropped across cable is 1.47 kV).\n \nc \nresponse to (b)(ii)\n260 A\n2 2\n\n«\n»\n«\n»  \n1 \n(continued…) \nAn approach from \n\n– 19 –\nM17/4/PHYSI/HP2/ENG/TZ2/XX/M \n(Question 6 continued) \nQuestion \nAnswers \nNotes \nTotal \n \nd \n \nwires/cable attract whenever current is in same direction  \nAward [1 max] if response suggests that there is \nrepulsion between cables at any stage in cycle. \n2 max \n \ncharge flow/current direction in both wires is always same «but \nreverses every half cycle»  \n \nforce varies from 0 to maximum  \n \nforce is a maximum twice in each cycle  \n \ne \ni \nhigher voltage gives lower current  \n2 \n \n«energy losses depend on current» hence thermal/heating/power \nlosse